<!doctype html>
<html lang="en">
<head>
  <meta charset="utf-8">
  <title>Cover Letter - Gravitee Engineering Manager</title>
  <style>
    @page { size: A4; margin: 22mm 22mm 22mm 22mm; }
    body {
      font-family: Arial, Helvetica, sans-serif;
      font-size: 11pt;
      line-height: 1.42;
      color: #111111;
      margin: 0;
    }
    .contact, .date, .salutation, .signoff { text-align: left; }
    .contact { margin-bottom: 18pt; }
    .date { margin-bottom: 18pt; }
    p {
      margin: 0 0 11pt 0;
      text-align: justify;
      text-justify: inter-word;
      hyphens: auto;
    }
    .salutation, .signoff { margin-top: 0; margin-bottom: 11pt; }
  </style>
</head>
<body>
  <div class="contact">
    Christos Hadjinikolis<br>
    christos.hadjinikolis@gmail.com<br>
    https://christos-hadjinikolis.github.io/<br>
    https://uk.linkedin.com/in/christoshadjinikolis
  </div>

  <div class="date">22 August 2026</div>

  <div class="salutation">Dear Gravitee hiring team,</div>

  <p>I am applying for the Engineering Manager role because the AI Agent Management problem looks like a serious infrastructure problem, not just an AI feature category. The part that interests me is the control layer around agentic systems: how APIs, events, tools, model calls, approvals, audit trails, and product interfaces become reliable enough for real organisations to use.</p>

  <p>My professional background is primarily in production ML, streaming systems, and engineering leadership rather than commercial API-management software. At Vortexa I manage six direct reports and lead a ten-person cross-functional team responsible for live model-backed backend systems. The estate processes roughly six million vessel-position records per hour into production intelligence for 13.5K monitored vessels, and the management problem is wider than model accuracy: product alignment, architecture, reliability, observability, delivery maturity, and clear interfaces between teams.</p>

  <p>That work has kept me close to the technical decisions. I have led Kafka/Flink streaming-platform evolution, model-serving APIs, MLflow-based lifecycle practices, evaluation and replay loops, CI/CD discipline, local integration tests, rollback/fallback paths, runbooks, shared on-call, and stakeholder workshops that turn ambiguous product/model questions into scoped workstreams. Earlier consulting work at Data Reply adds client-facing delivery, GCP/Kubeflow MLOps platform work, predictive-maintenance leadership, and real-time Kafka/Elasticsearch pipelines.</p>

  <p>The newer agent-specific part of the story comes from recent personal and open-source work. Promet is a private local-first AI harness where I have worked directly on MCP connector setup, tool routing, schema validation, side-effect policy, approval gates, OAuth-backed connector boundaries, durable state, traces, and replay. Skeleton and the Skeleton Replay IDE plugin came out of the same concern: AI-assisted development needs runtime evidence that both humans and LLMs can inspect, not only faster code generation.</p>

  <p>I treat that as current, hands-on agent-infrastructure work rather than a substitute for commercial API-management product experience. The credible connection is that Gravitee is applying API and event-management discipline to agentic systems, and my career has repeatedly been about turning fast-moving AI or data capabilities into dependable software that engineers can operate, evaluate, and improve. That is a coherent next step for me, and a technically demanding place to bring engineering management, distributed-systems judgement, and current hands-on agent-infrastructure work together.</p>

  <div class="signoff">
    Sincerely,<br><br>
    Christos Hadjinikolis
  </div>
</body>
</html>
